\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces Two ways to use a model. In a workflow (a), code decides the order of the steps and the model fills in some of them. In an agent (b), the model decides which tool to use next, reads the result, and decides again, until it chooses to stop. Dashed arrows are possibilities, not a fixed path.}}{10}{figure.1.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:workflow-vs-agent}{{1.5}{10}{Two ways to use a model. In a workflow (a), code decides the order of the steps and the model fills in some of them. In an agent (b), the model decides which tool to use next, reads the result, and decides again, until it chooses to stop. Dashed arrows are possibilities, not a fixed path}{figure.1.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces The autonomy spectrum. Each row down the table gives the model more control over what happens next. The shading runs from green, which is predictable, cheap and easy to test, to red, which is flexible but harder to test and more expensive to run.}}{11}{table.1.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:autonomy}{{1.3}{11}{The autonomy spectrum. Each row down the table gives the model more control over what happens next. The shading runs from green, which is predictable, cheap and easy to test, to red, which is flexible but harder to test and more expensive to run}{table.1.3}{}}
